window.docContents[176].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity--Interior-Derivative-Estimates--Derivative-estimates-and-Liouville___s-theorem":{"contents":"Interior derivative estimates express the rigidity of harmonic functions\nquantitatively: the derivatives of  at a point are controlled by the size of\n on any surrounding sphere or ball. The proof identifies  with the\nPoisson extension of its boundary trace and differentiates the Poisson kernel\nat the center, which yields the constant  without assuming in advance\nthat the derivatives are harmonic.\n\nIn positive dimension, suppose , ,  is harmonic on a set\ncontaining the closed ball of radius  about , and\n on the boundary sphere. Then\n.\n\nTranslate the ball to the origin, extend the boundary trace, and identify the\ntranslated function locally with its Poisson extension.  Differentiating the\nPoisson kernel at the center and estimating the resulting spherical integral\nby Cauchy–Schwarz gives the explicit constant .\n\nReplacing the boundary supremum by an integral of  costs the volume of a\nhalf-radius ball: the boundary estimate is applied on , and  at\neach point of its sphere is bounded by the ball average of  over a ball of\nradius  that still lies inside . The constant\n produced this way is Evans's.\n\nIn positive dimension, if  and  is harmonic on a set containing\n, thenwhere  is the volume of the unit ball. The bound is for the\noperator norm of the Fréchet derivative, so it bounds each coordinate\nderivative.\n\nApply the boundary-supremum estimate on the half-radius ball.  At each point\nof its boundary sphere, apply the ball mean-value formula on the ball of\nradius  centered there. These smaller open balls lie in ;\nthe triangle inequality, monotonicity of the integral of , and\n give Evans's constant.\n\nIterating the first-order estimate bounds derivatives of every order, and each\niteration spends part of the available radius: to control  at , one\nestimates  on a sphere of radius  about , each point of\nwhich must still have a ball of radius  inside the domain.\nThe sum of radii records this nested geometry.\n\nIn positive dimension, let  be harmonic on an open set , let \nbe any natural number, and assign a positive radius budget  to each\nderivative step. IfthenTaking every  gives the uniform estimate on\n with denominator . In particular,  and\n recover the usual second- and third-derivative nested-ball estimates.\nThe order-zero case is included.\n\nFirst derive harmonicity of the evaluated derivatives from harmonicity of\n on the open domain. Then induct on , spending the head radius in\nthe final first-derivative estimate. On its boundary sphere, the induction\nhypothesis controls the preceding iterated derivative using the tail radii.\nApply the first-order estimate to each multilinear evaluation and use the\noperator norm to reassemble the directional bounds. Constant radius budgets\ngive the uniform theorem; replacing  by  gives the successor form.\n\nLiouville's theorem is the limiting case of the boundary-supremum estimate on\nall of : if  everywhere, then\n for every , so  vanishes identically\nand  is constant.\n\nIn positive dimension, if  is harmonic and\nglobally bounded, then\n\nApply the boundary-supremum derivative estimate on\narbitrarily large balls.  If the derivative norm were positive, choosing a\nradius larger than its bound would give a strict contradiction.  Mathlib's\nzero-derivative constancy theorem then identifies every value with the value\nat the origin.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Model Dirichlet Problems and Harmonic Rigidity\u0009Interior Derivative Estimates","header":"5.3.3.1. Derivative estimates and Liouville's theorem","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity--Interior-Derivative-Estimates--Derivative-estimates-and-Liouville___s-theorem"},"/Related-Work/#PDEAtlas--Related-Work--Computation-with-a-mathematical-conclusion":{"contents":"Verified computer algebra has its own history in Lean. Lewis and Wu's\nLean–Mathematica interface exchanges expressions in both directions and\nreconstructs Lean proofs for selected computations returned by Mathematica\n. The external algebra system acts as an\nuntrusted source of candidates. This distinction also matters for an\nantiderivative: finding a formula and proving that its derivative equals the\nintegrand, on the required domain, are separate steps.\n\nHex provides a more recent comparison inside Lean itself. Its released libraries\ncover verified computational algebra, including polynomial algorithms, lattice\nbasis reduction, and algebraic numbers, with separate Mathlib correspondence\nlayers where available . This exact algebra supports\ncomputations with finite mathematical objects. PDEAtlas connects symbolic\ndifferentiation, supported antiderivatives, and certified enclosures to analytic\nfunctions and PDE residuals. Its procedures return theorems over the analytic\ndefinitions used by the surrounding proofs, with domain and regularity\nconditions retained in the statements. A residual calculation can then become\na proved identity inside an analytic argument.\n\nValidated numerics contributes a complementary guarantee. Boldo, Clément,\nFilliâtre, Mayero, Melquiond, and Weis verified a C implementation of a\none-dimensional acoustic wave scheme, accounting for both discretization and\nfloating-point roundoff errors\n. Their analysis connects the program,\nthe discrete scheme, and the continuous equation. The distinction explains why\ncertifying arithmetic alone cannot establish the accuracy of a PDE solver:\nstability and approximation estimates must connect those levels.\n\nThe Rocq Interval library automates bounds on real expressions and integrals\n. In Lean, LeanCert turns interval,\nroot, and integral certificates into mathematical conclusions\n. Its interval and quadrature tools overlap\nclosely with CEGA: a finite calculation produces a bound that an analytic proof\ncan use. In PDEAtlas, CEGA keeps the function and its domain attached to that\nbound, so an estimate for a residual on one box can feed into a larger PDE\nargument. LeanCert offers kernel reduction and a default native evaluation\nroute; the latter additionally relies on Lean's compiler and runtime.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Related Work","header":"2.4. Computation with a mathematical conclusion","id":"/Related-Work/#PDEAtlas--Related-Work--Computation-with-a-mathematical-conclusion"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#certified-plots":{"contents":"The Gaussian kernel, traveling sine, and Burgers profile have already served\nas examples of PDE identities. We return to them here to ask a different\nquestion: which values can each function take throughout a small spatial\ninterval? The enclosure machinery answers that question with a theorem for\neach panel. The horizontal axis is cut into forty\nrational panels, and a theorem places each function between two rational\nendpoints throughout every panel. The theorem is proved by\nthe cega tactic from an interval certificate and checked by Lean's kernel\nwhen the site is built. What is drawn is exactly the union of those certified\nboxes: three explicit solutions from the parabolic and hyperbolic chapters, as\nthe calculator certified them.\n\nHow to read a plot. Each colour is one value of the time parameter . Each\nshaded panel is one theorem: its horizontal extent is the panel  of\nthe -axis and its vertical extent is the certified range , so\nthe true curve runs inside the shaded strip. Each strip covers the function's\nvariation over the panel and may also include overestimation from the enclosure\ncalculation. Its endpoints are rounded outward to six decimal places so that\nthe statements stay readable.\n\nSelecting a panel opens its row in the theorem listing under the plot. Each\nrow gives the panel, the certified range, and the full Lean statement, of the\nform\n\nEnclosedOn (fun p : ℝ^1 ↦ Real.sin (p 0 - 1))\n  {p | 0 ≤ p 0 ∧ p 0 ≤ 3/20} (-841519/1000000) (-375639/500000)\n\n\nread \"for every point  of the one-dimensional Euclidean space with\n, the value  lies between the two\nrationals\". The theorem name links to its line in the generated module, which\nthe Blueprint build compiles. The generator asks the calculator for every\ncertified range before writing the theorems, and it checks that the drawing\nand the theorems list the same panels and bounds.\n\nThe first plot is the one-dimensional heat kernel\n\nat ,  and  on . Its total mass is one at\nevery time, so the curve spreads and flattens as  grows. The panels are\nnarrow strips near the tails, where the kernel is nearly flat, and taller\naround the flanks of the peak. The certificates handle the real power\n, the exponential and the constant  together; the\nGaussian-kernel section of the parabolic chapter proves the kernel's\nproperties as theorems and the textbook coverage map\nlocates them in Evans's §2.3.\n\n\n\nThe second plot is d'Alembert's traveling sine wave \nat ,  and  on . The wave moves to the\nright with unit speed, so the three curves are translates of one another by\none half. The function is the one whose wave-equation residual is checked in\nthe\nwave and transport example.\n\n\n\nThe third plot is the self-similar Burgers profile  at\n,  and  on . This is the formula used\ninside a rarefaction fan. The\nfull Riemann solution restricts\nit to the region between the rays determined by the two states and joins it\nto constant values outside. The plot encloses only the fan formula, on a\nfixed window, without the outer states. The\nprofile is increasing and linear in  on every panel, so its exact range is\ndetermined by its endpoint values. The strips show how the fan opens with\ntime: the same window of  covers a smaller range of velocities at later\ntimes.\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation","header":"10.12. Certified Plots","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/#certified-plots"}});